---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import CourseInfoList from "../../components/CourseInfoList.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../../course-config";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";
import "../../styles/photo.css";
---

<ContentLayout title="Course" description={courseMeta.description} {...siteConfig}>
  <section aria-labelledby="course-overview">
    <h2 id="course-overview">Course Overview</h2>
    <p>
      {courseMeta.title} is not a commercial portrait or model-photography
      course. It has one central aim: learning how to photograph your own
      girlfriend in a way she genuinely likes. Every topic is taught in service
      of making photographs she will want to keep and share.
    </p>
    <p>
      The course follows the complete process before, during and after a shoot.
      Before taking photos, students learn how their girlfriend likes to be
      photographed, then plan the date, location, light and equipment. While
      photographing, they learn to recognise the right moment, compose, guide
      and adapt. Afterwards, they review the images with her, compare choices,
      agree on editing boundaries and finish the photographs together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Learning how to photograph during a date supports this process. Comfort,
      trust, timing and mood shape how your girlfriend looks and feels in front
      of the camera, helping produce an image she will want to keep.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="course-details">
    <h2 id="course-details">Course Details</h2>
    <CourseInfoList />
    <p>
      For attendance, academic integrity, extensions and support, see the
      <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies and support page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="teaching-format">
    <h2 id="teaching-format">Teaching Format</h2>
    <p>
      All teaching is delivered in person on campus. Each week combines a
      face-to-face lecture with a practical session built around photographing,
      reviewing or planning with classmates. These exercises develop methods
      students can apply when photographing their girlfriends. There is no
      online teaching stream; material on this website supports preparation and
      revision but does not replace participation in class.
    </p>
    <p>
      Students should bring a phone or camera when requested and arrive ready
      to alternate between photographer, portrait partner and reviewer.
      Exercises are designed around communication and consent as much as camera
      technique.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="learning-outcomes">
    <h2 id="learning-outcomes">Learning Outcomes</h2>
    <p>By the end of this course, students will be able to:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        plan short photographic moments within a date around shared intent,
        consent, location, available light, weather and their girlfriend's
        energy;
      </li>
      <li>
        understand and apply their girlfriend's preferences through a reusable
        Partner Photography Profile;
      </li>
      <li>
        use camera height, working distance, focal length, framing and available
        light to make deliberate portrait decisions;
      </li>
      <li>
        guide their girlfriend through actions, movement and conversation while
        maintaining comfort and trust; and
      </li>
      <li>
        compare their own selections with their girlfriend's, agree on editing
        boundaries and finish photographs she genuinely wants to keep and share.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="course-philosophy">
    <h2 id="course-philosophy">Course Philosophy</h2>
    <p>
      Your girlfriend's response is the primary measure of success. A
      technically impressive or artistically ambitious portrait has still
      missed the point if she does not like how she is seen in it. The
      photographer's taste matters, but it does not overrule the person the
      photograph is made for.
    </p>
    <p>
      The experience of the date matters because it shapes that photograph.
      Attention, comfort and trust make genuine expression possible; pressure
      and over-direction work against it. Knowing how to prepare, when to take
      the camera out and when to put it down is therefore part of learning how
      to make an image your girlfriend will love.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="course-structure">
    <h2 id="course-structure">Course Structure</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/weeks/")}>twelve-week sequence</a> develops in
      five stages:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Weeks 1–2 — Foundations and planning.</strong> Establish what
        makes a photograph feel successful to your girlfriend, then plan how
        photography will fit into the date: intention, consent, timing,
        location, weather and a realistic shooting window.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Week 3 — Equipment and lenses.</strong> Learn what phones and
        cameras do differently, how focal length and working distance change a
        face, and how to choose practical equipment for the date rather than
        carrying everything available.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weeks 4–8 — Core photographic skills.</strong> Build control of
        composition, background, camera height, perspective, exposure, focus,
        movement and available light through repeated portrait exercises.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weeks 9–10 — Communication and direction.</strong> Learn how to
        ask what your girlfriend likes, give clear prompts, respond to comfort
        and confidence, and guide expression without making her perform as a
        professional model.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weeks 11–12 — Selection and post-production.</strong> Compare
        both people's shortlists, agree on cropping and retouching boundaries,
        then complete colour, skin tone, export and sharing decisions together.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Weekly <a href={withBase("/resources/")}>resources</a> support preparation
      and revision. Three <a href={withBase("/assessments/")}>assessment</a>
      milestones track the move from individual decisions to a complete set of
      photographs chosen and approved together.
    </p>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
